Describe the complete sequence to run a script “worker.sh” in the background, check
that it is running, observe its resource usage, and then terminate it gracefully. Include
all commands in the correct order.

chmod +x worker.sh
./worker.sh &
PID=$!
echo $PID
jobs -l
ps -fp $PID
top -p $PID
ps -o pid,%cpu,%mem,rss,vsz,etime,stat,cmd -p $PID
kill -SIGTERM $PID
ps -p $PID
jobs -l
kill -SIGKILL $PID    # only if it ignored SIGTERM


Explain the difference between the “SIGTERM” and “SIGKILL” signals. Why is it
generally preferable to try “SIGTERM” first when stopping a process?

SIGTERM tries to safely terminate the target process while SIGKILL immediately stops the target process regardless of what might happen.


